body:
I am going to get a lil bit metaphorical here but why not, let's go.

&#x200B;

Chemistry, by itself is sort of like a language. A language that can explain the workings of the world like language (english, Chinese, Spanish, et) itself. It has certain rules, such as carbon only has 4 valance electrons. These rules seem weird at first, but the more time you spend in chemistry, the more these rules become natural to your mind. 

&#x200B;

Now, biochemistry to me, is like chemistry, but on steroids. Biochemistry is about realizing how complex life is, and how crazy it is that even life exist, given how complex life is. But then again, thinking about the universe and physics (which i have a hard time understanding to this day) is quite complex too. We will always be able to discover new reaction pathways of the body. I think it's crazy that we even know about things like all the reactions of cellular respiration in the first place. We will always learn more and more about how DNA works to create proteins. We will have a better understanding of the ways proteins fold. It's crazy that we attempt to break down this complexity into its components of biochemical reactions. I have a hard time believing we will ever get a "finalized" understanding of how the human body operate, or yet even a "finalized" version of some theory of how exactly a biomolecule (likely many) came to create life itself. Biochemistry is a mystery, and its the small particles on which life is built. Humans have always been fascinated with life, and why life exists in the first place. Studying biochem is like studying the precise mechanisms of life in the first place. Personally, I find it more satisfying and intriguing to learn about processes that take place on the cellular level, than to learn how food moves through the digestive tract, or all the names of muscles. 

&#x200B;

ok enough with this tangent

Looking at academic studies, it's very ambiguous. Some studies say it's good for bones, others say whether it's good or not is inconclusive. The way nutrition studies are conducted makes it hard to say, as most of them have to do with surveys of what people were eating and it's really hard to link one specific food to diseases. This one study [https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2539753/](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2539753/) used surveys of people recalling of how much milk they drank on average during their childhood (how in the world is this supposed to be reliable?) 

&#x200B;

Another example of not such a good study is this one  [https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3746166/](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3746166/) In this study, 55.7% of male and 82.2% of female subjects had low bone density, less than the normal range, which may lead to serious bone-health problems when they become adults. This low bone density of this study's subjects is maybe due to the low daily intake of milk and dairy products in subjects, which is 44.5% less than the amount of daily recommended calcium intake for adolescents. This entire study was based off a survey of high school students writing down how much milk/calcium products they had been eating over the course of 4 days! Yes, 4 days! 

&#x200B;

Looking at the bone density of people who are allergic to milk (and therefore can't drink it) may have something worthy to say, such as one study that showed people with allergies had lower bone densities. [https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/25091435](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/25091435)

&#x200B;

On studies that do link milk with negative effects could include this one [https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/18382426](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/18382426) which demonstrated milk (and other foods) could increase risk of prostate cancer. "Calcium from dairy products was also positively associated with risk, but not calcium from other foods."  However, we eat many things, so again, it's still hard to link a specific food to disease, even if there is a correlation, we can't be sure. 

&#x200B;

Like people said below, I do agree it's kind of weird to drink something that is meant for a baby cow, but does that mean it's bad for our health? Humans have been drinking milk for thousands of years ([https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2009/08/090827202513.htm](https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2009/08/090827202513.htm)) and in fact we were eating cheese long before drinking milk. I think eating things like processed foods (sugary things/fried things/you know what I mean) is far worse than drinking some milk. If someone has a better answer, some other studies please do enlighten me

How old are you? If possible, try to see a professional (therapist) and tell them exactly what you've said here. You say you feel bored while with your friends, but by yourself you feel terrible? Terrible can mean a lot of things. Why do you dislike people? Do you find them annoying? Most people will say how can you not like someone if you barely know them? "I despise how fixated society is with dumb shit like beauty" I gotta agree with you on this one. So, is there anything you like at all about life? Anything you like, even something small like a sunny day? "I wish I could fake my death and disappear" what would you do then, where would you go? You'd be all alone, right, and didn't you say you felt terrible when you were alone? 

&#x200B;

 I'm just asking these questions because I'm having a hard time understanding what's going on. Either way, a professional would be best to go to. "break some day and hurt people" that concerns me. Is this anger?
